module stdlib.fs

# Filesystem primitives. Declared here rather than in the prelude: nothing in the prelude calls
# them, and under the rule "an extern lives in the module that owns its surface" this is that
# module. Touching the filesystem becomes an explicit dependency — the same reasoning the prelude
# already records for stdlib.net's socket primitives and stdlib.terminal's control codes.
#
# `export` on an `extern fn` would be inert: the parser discards it (parser.sprout:1941) and the
# bundler never registers extern names (bundler.sprout:597), so an extern is reachable by bare name
# from anywhere this module is bundled. The wrapper API below is what makes that reach intentional
# and greppable, not a visibility rule.
#
# NO TYPE DECLARATIONS IN THIS MODULE, deliberately. The REPL's module loader validates type-decl
# field names against a fixed vocabulary and swallows the error when one is missing, which is why
# eleven stdlib modules currently fail to load there (docs/repl-env-type-vocabulary-v0.md §1.2).
# Function signatures are not scanned, so a module of externs and wrappers loads cleanly. Adding a
# `type` here would silently drop stdlib.fs out of the REPL.
extern fn read_file(path: String) -> Result String String !{IO}
extern fn write_file(path: String, content: String) -> Result String Unit !{IO}

# The names say `text`, not `file`, because that is the real contract. `read_file` runs
# `utf8_validate` over the whole buffer before it hands anything back (sprout_runtime.c:2984) and
# reports the decode failure as `Err` — so this pair genuinely cannot read a binary file, and the
# name should say so rather than implying a general reader. It also leaves honest room for a
# bytes-valued pair later; stdlib.process reached exactly that point with subprocess output (see
# its header comment) once holding binary data in a `String` proved unusable.
#
# `Err` is a human-readable message, not a matchable code: `strerror(errno)` for an I/O failure,
# the utf8_validate reason for a non-text file, or `"null path"` / `"out of memory"`. Do not
# pattern-match its content.
export fn read_text(path: String) -> Result String String !{IO} =
  read_file(path)

export fn write_text(path: String, content: String) -> Result String Unit !{IO} =
  write_file(path, content)
